, {"t": "md", "lp": -0.45919644832611084, "top": [["md", -0.45919644832611084], ["txt", -1.3817753791809082]]}, {"t": "\"", "lp": -0.0008773478912189603, "top": [["\"", -0.0008773478912189603], ["*\"", -7.864129066467285]]}, {"t": " ", "lp": -0.09787815809249878, "top": [[" ", -0.09787815809249878], [" -", -2.374117612838745]]}, {"t": "2", "lp": -0.0003196511825080961, "top": [["2", -0.0003196511825080961], [".", -11.825318336486816]]}, {"t": ">/", "lp": -0.00034624370164237916, "top": [[">/", -0.00034624370164237916], ["=/", -10.856850624084473]]}, {"t": "dev", "lp": -2.9325914510991424e-05, "top": [["dev", -2.9325914510991424e-05], [" dev", -12.534588813781738]]}, {"t": "/null", "lp": -2.5868750526569784e-05, "top": [["/null", -2.5868750526569784e-05], [" null", -11.973898887634277]]}, {"t": " |", "lp": -0.000506171490997076, "top": [[" |", -0.000506171490997076], ["\n", -8.6333646774292]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Command Streams (CCS) are allocated on your GPU, which affects how work is distributed across the hardware's EU (Execution Unit) slices.\n\n## The Modes\n\n**ccs_mode 1**\n- Uses the **default** CCS allocation determined by the driver at runtime\n- The xe driver automatically figures out which slice(s) should host the CCS based on the system's topology\n- This is the \"just works\" setting for most users\n\n**ccs_mode 2**\n- Uses a **specific** CCS configuration, typically pinning work to a particular slice or set of slices\n- More explicit control over which slice handles the command stream\n\n**ccs_mode 4**\n- Typically corresponds to a **different** distribution strategy (often per-slice or a specific mapping)\n\n## When to Use Each\n\nThe exact semantics can be a bit murky because:\n\n1. **The xe driver's `ccs_mode` handling has evolved** across kernel versions, and the precise meaning of each numeric value isn't consistently documented across releases.\n2. **Different versions may map numbers differently** \u2014 what's \"mode 1\" in one kernel might differ in another.\n\n## Practical Guidance\n\nFor most users:\n- **Leave it at the default (mode 1)** unless you're debugging specific performance issues\n- The default is generally well-optimized for the A770's 16 EU slices\n\n## Getting the Exact Answer\n\nBecause the numeric mapping is version-specific, I'd recommend checking your **actual kernel version's documentation**:\n\n```bash\n# Check your driver/kernel version\ndmesg | grep -i xe\nuname -r\n\n# Look for the parameter documentation\nfind / -name \"*.md\" 2>/dev/null |"}